\title{catchmentACS: Isochrone-Based Area-Weighted ACS Aggregation}
\description{
catchmentACS computes estimates for drive-time areas (isochrones) around
sites, such as Pre-K classrooms, by combining the American Community
Survey (ACS) 5-year estimates of the census tracts that overlap each area.
The tract estimates are weighted by area, and \code{\link[=cacs_intersect_weight]{cacs_intersect_weight()}}
describes the weights and what they assume. The five rates in
\code{\link{cacs_acs_default_rates}} are ratios of the weighted counts. Each estimate
has a margin of error, the half-width of its confidence interval (90
percent by default). \code{\link[=cacs_propagate_moe]{cacs_propagate_moe()}} and \code{\link[=cacs_derive_rates]{cacs_derive_rates()}}
describe how the margins of error are computed and what they assume. Every
result carries a record of the settings and inputs behind it, which
\code{\link[=cacs_describe]{cacs_describe()}} prints.
}
\details{
\code{\link[=cacs_run]{cacs_run()}} runs five steps in one call, and each step can also be called
on its own:
\enumerate{
\item \code{\link[=cacs_acs_prefetch]{cacs_acs_prefetch()}} downloads the ACS data for one state with
tidycensus, which needs a Census API key.
\item \code{\link[=cacs_isochrone]{cacs_isochrone()}} builds the drive-time areas with the Open Source
Routing Machine (OSRM, the default) or openrouteservice, which needs an
API key.
\item \code{\link[=cacs_intersect_weight]{cacs_intersect_weight()}} combines the tract estimates for each area.
\item \code{\link[=cacs_propagate_moe]{cacs_propagate_moe()}} computes the margins of error at a chosen
confidence level.
\item \code{\link[=cacs_derive_rates]{cacs_derive_rates()}} computes the rates and their margins of error.
}

\code{\link[=cacs_run]{cacs_run()}} skips the download when ACS data are supplied through \code{acs},
and the routing when drive-time areas are supplied through
\code{precomputed_isochrones}. The \code{provider} values \code{"mapbox"} and \code{"r5r"} and
the \code{weight_method} value \code{"population"} are accepted but not implemented
yet. Building drive-time areas with Mapbox or r5r, or weighting by
population, gives an error.

